::CW_U02_L01_GP_Q01::Which choice best identifies a narrative technique in this sentence? "Maya pressed her ear to the door and held her breath."{
~A. Dialogue#Incorrect. Dialogue means exact spoken words. This sentence does not show anyone speaking.
~B. Reflection#Incorrect. Reflection shows a thought, realization, or interpretation. This sentence focuses on what Maya does.
=C. Action#Correct. Maya presses her ear to the door and holds her breath. These are actions that show the reader what she is doing.
~D. Transition#Incorrect. A transition shows movement between moments, such as time, cause, place, or contrast. This sentence is not mainly connecting two moments.
}
::CW_U02_L01_GP_Q02::Which revision best turns the event list into a scene? Event list: "I opened the box. I saw the medal. I cried."{
=A. I lifted the cardboard flap and found the silver medal wrapped in a blue ribbon. For a moment, I could not move. Then my eyes filled with tears.#Correct. This revision uses action, description, and a time transition to let the reader experience the moment.
~B. I opened the box, saw the medal, and cried.#Incorrect. This keeps the event list compressed. It tells what happened but does not develop the moment as a scene.
~C. First, I opened the box. Next, I saw the medal. Finally, I cried.#Incorrect. This adds mechanical transitions, but it still reads like a list rather than a developed scene.
~D. The medal was important because I cried after opening the box.#Incorrect. This explains the event instead of showing it through narrative techniques.
}
::CW_U02_L01_GP_Q03::Which transition best shows cause in this sentence? "_____ the hallway lights went out, Jordan reached for the wall."{
~A. Across from#Incorrect. "Across from" shows place, not cause.
~B. Later that day#Incorrect. "Later that day" shows time, not cause.
~C. Meanwhile#Incorrect. "Meanwhile" shows simultaneous action, not cause.
=D. Because#Correct. "Because the hallway lights went out" explains the cause of Jordan reaching for the wall.
}
::CW_U02_L01_GP_Q04::A student writes, "First, I ran. Next, I hid. Then, I waited." What is the strongest feedback?{
~A. Add more first, next, and then transitions so the sequence is clearer.#Incorrect. The sequence is already clear, but the writing is still mechanical and underdeveloped.
=B. Replace some mechanical transitions with action, description, or cause transitions that show why the character runs, hides, and waits.#Correct. Strong feedback points to a specific revision that improves scene development and reader understanding.
~C. Remove all transitions because stories should not use transitions.#Incorrect. Stories need transitions, but transitions should show meaningful relationships between moments.
~D. Keep the exact sentence because it already has three transitions.#Incorrect. The sentence has transitions, but it does not use action, description, or cause-and-effect movement well.
}
::CW_U02_L01_GP_Q05::Which explanation correctly describes why this revision works? Draft: "The phone rang. I saw the name. I ignored it." Revision: "When the phone rang, I glanced at the screen. My brother's name flashed there, bright as a warning, so I pushed the phone under my pillow."{
~A. It works because every sentence starts with the same word.#Incorrect. Repetition is not what makes this revision effective.
~B. It works because it removes all transitions.#Incorrect. The revision includes transitions such as "When" and "so."
=C. It works because action, description, and cause transitions show the narrator's reaction more clearly.#Correct. The revision uses action, description, and cause to help the reader understand the moment.
~D. It works because it explains the entire backstory before the action begins.#Incorrect. The revision does not explain the entire backstory. It focuses on a specific moment.
}
